1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,762
Day 49: OpenWorker, Andrew Ng's AI coworker,

2
00:00:03,762 --> 00:00:08,208
run headless on a local model with no cloud API key.

3
00:00:08,658 --> 00:00:13,104
OpenWorker is a coworker agent that works in your files and

4
00:00:13,104 --> 00:00:13,556
tools.

5
00:00:13,556 --> 00:00:16,720
By design, every tool call needs approval,

6
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:19,583
and every call gets logged either way.

7
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:24,853
We ran the CLI headless, isolated from any real settings,

8
00:00:24,853 --> 00:00:29,249
pointed at a local llama.cpp server serving Qwen2.5,

9
00:00:29,249 --> 00:00:31,109
no cloud key involved.

10
00:00:31,559 --> 00:00:34,516
When the model calls a tool like write_file,

11
00:00:34,516 --> 00:00:36,263
the approval gate decides:

12
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:38,817
in ask mode with no terminal attached,

13
00:00:38,817 --> 00:00:40,766
it is refused before it runs.

14
00:00:41,216 --> 00:00:43,724
Either way, approved or denied,

15
00:00:43,724 --> 00:00:48,336
the call lands in a SQLite audit log: proposed, resolved,

16
00:00:48,336 --> 00:00:49,064
finished.

17
00:00:49,514 --> 00:00:53,169
In ask mode, a write_file call was denied and logged,

18
00:00:53,169 --> 00:00:55,099
with brief.md never created.

19
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:57,719
With approvals bypassed, the call ran,

20
00:00:57,719 --> 00:01:01,788
but a 3B model's two read_file calls both hit a wrong path,

21
00:01:01,788 --> 00:01:06,063
and it wrote a placeholder brief without ever reading the real

22
00:01:06,063 --> 00:01:06,476
notes.

23
00:01:06,926 --> 00:01:09,600
The approval gate worked exactly as documented.

24
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,126
Whether the model behind it could be trusted with the task was

25
00:01:13,126 --> 00:01:14,264
a separate question.
